C = [
("h1", "12. Objections and Replies"),
("p", "Claim: the volume’s reading, that Malachi promises one Elijah, that John came in his spirit and power and was Elias if the nation would receive it, and that the text points to a further coming before the day, answers the objections best made against it, and where an objector is right the volume says so. Modern writers who hold parts of the position are named beside those who press against it. A writer is quoted only where the author has read the words in a free text. A writer’s view is described in the volume’s own words when only a summary of it was read, and no quotation marks are used for it."),
("sub", "12.1 Who agrees, and where the volume differs"),
("p", "Three modern writers hold that Malachi’s promise has a coming still ahead. Thomas Ice, in an article titled “Elijah is Coming” published by the Pre-Trib Research Center in 2018, writes, “So Elijah is still to come.” He holds that the promise was offered to Israel in John’s day and not taken up. His sentence, “John the Baptist fulfilled a different prediction, that of Malachi 3:1 and Isaiah 40:3-5,” keeps the two sayings of chapter 4 apart, as this volume does. He also writes that “because of Israel’s rejection, John the Baptist was John the Baptist (My messenger) and not Elijah.” The volume agrees on the offer and its refusal and on the two sayings. It differs in two respects. Ice places the coming in the tribulation, and this volume says only that the coming is before the day of the LORD, and it does not name the period. And Ice’s article reads the coming of Elijah as a visit by Elijah himself, while the volume leaves open whether the one who comes is the Tishbite or a man in his spirit."),
("p", "Walter C. Kaiser Jr., in an article in //Grace Theological Journal// in 1982 titled “The Promise of the Arrival of Elijah in Malachi and the Gospels,” sets out three ways to read the promise. The first is that John fully fulfilled it. The second is that the Tishbite returns in person. The third is that John came in Elijah’s spirit and power as one of a series of forerunners. Kaiser holds the third. He writes that “John then was Elijah as an earnest, but we still await the other Elijahs.” He also argues that a present-tense verb in the Lord’s sentence is, in his words, a “futuristic present,” because “this present is coupled with a future tense.” The volume agrees that John came as an earnest, to use Kaiser’s word, and that the Lord’s sentences look ahead. It differs in not adopting his series. The volume asks what the Gospels say about the Elijah of Malachi 4:5 and holds one further coming before the day; it does not say that Scripture lists a line of forerunners."),
("p", "Paul Henebury, in an article titled “John the Baptist and Elijah” on the SharperIron site in 2021, grants the point many make and denies the conclusion drawn from it. He writes, “Many scholars have argued that the fulfillment of the Elijah prophecy (of Mal. 4:5), is to be confined to John the Baptist.” His answer is, “But this does not force us to conclude that there is no future work for Elijah to do.” He goes on to write, “At the second coming of Messiah as Conqueror and King, Elijah himself returns to prepare the way.” The volume agrees that a work remains. It differs by not tying the coming to the Messiah’s return as a date, since no passage says so, and by not saying that the person is Elijah himself. The three writers support the shape of the position and none of them is quoted for more than they say."),
("sub", "12.2 Objection: Malachi speaks of an office, and John filled it"),
("p", "The strongest opposing reading holds that Malachi’s Elijah is John. Angus Stewart, in an address titled “The Coming of Elijah” given at the Covenant Protestant Reformed Church in 2004, writes, “Elijah in our text is John the Baptist, not the literal Tishbite revived.” His reason is that “Malachi 4:5 speaks of Elijah’s office rather than His person,” and he adds, “Similarly, Malachi is not prophesying that Elijah will return and labour again.” His sentence of the pattern is “Rather, one like Elijah will do a work like Elijah.” An unsigned devotional in the Ligonier Ministries series //Tabletalk// takes the same side. It writes, “However, Malachi did not predict a return of the very same Elijah in the flesh.” The objection is that the promise is met by a man in Elijah’s office, and John was that man."),
("p", "Reply. The volume grants three things. First, a man in Elijah’s office or in his spirit and power is what Luke 1:17 says of John, and the volume has read it so. Second, the promise does not require that the Tishbite himself come, and the volume does not say that he does. Third, the devotional is right about the people of John’s day: it writes, “The Jews were expecting a literal return of Elijah, and in that respect, John the Baptist certainly was not Elijah,” which is what John says in John 1:21. The volume parts from the objection at one point. The office reading says that the promise is closed because John held the office. The Lord’s sentence, “Elias truly shall first come, and restore all things” (Matthew 17:11), is spoken after John’s death, and the restoring is not reported done. If the office was filled by John and the promise closed, the future tense of the Lord’s sentence has no work to do. A man may fill an office in the sense of Matthew 11:14 and the office may be filled again before the day."),
("sub", "12.3 Objection: “Elias is come already” settles it"),
("p", "The objection reads the second sentence of Matthew 17:12 as the Lord’s last word: Elias is come already, the disciples understood that he spake of John, and the matter is closed. Mark’s form, “Elias is indeed come” (Mark 9:13), is read the same way. Reply. The sentence is the second of a pair, and the pair begins, “Elias truly shall first come, and restore all things.” The Lord does not say that Elias has come and that the scribes are therefore wrong. He says that the scribes’ teaching is true, and then he says “But I say unto you” that Elias is come already and they knew him not. A reader who takes only the second sentence takes half of what the Lord said, and a reader who takes only the first takes the other half. The volume holds both in the order in which they were spoken. Matthew 17:13 says that the disciples understood that he spake unto them of John, and the volume reports that the verse comes after both sentences."),
("sub", "12.4 Objection: a further coming adds what Jesus did not say"),
("p", "A reader may say that the volume adds to the Gospels a coming the Lord never described. No writer is named for this; it is the volume’s own statement of the worry. Reply. The Lord said that Elias “shall first come, and restore all things,” and the volume takes the sentence at its word. What the volume adds is two observations drawn from the text: the sentence was spoken after John’s death, and the restoring is not reported done. It adds no date, no place, no name, no description of the coming beyond what Malachi and the Lord say. The charge of adding applies with equal force to a reading that says the promise is closed, for that reading adds that the future tense of Matthew 17:11 is a figure for something already past."),
("sub", "12.5 Objection: “restore all things” is John’s preaching"),
("p", "Another reader may say that “restore all things” describes what John’s preaching did, calling the nation to repentance. No writer is named. Reply. The Gospels describe John’s preaching as a call to repent, with crowds going out to him (Matthew 3:5, 6), and they describe the leaders refusing it (Luke 7:30), and the Lord says that “this generation” refused both John and the Son of man (Matthew 11:16–19). They do not report that all things were restored. Peter, after the cross, speaks of restitution as ahead, “until the times of restitution of all things” (Acts 3:21). The volume takes the Lord’s phrase to be as wide as it sounds, and reports that the Gospels do not say it was met."),
("sub", "12.6 Objection: John 1:21 and Matthew 11:14 contradict"),
("p", "A last objection says that if John says “I am not” and the Lord says “this is Elias,” one of them is wrong. No writer is named. Reply. The two statements are each made in a setting. John answers priests and Levites of the Pharisees who ask a question about a figure they expected (John 1:19–25). The Lord speaks to the multitudes and makes his statement conditional: “if ye will receive it” (Matthew 11:14). The volume has not argued that the two statements are the same statement. It has said that neither is made to say more than it says, and that neither needs to be rewritten for the reading to stand. If the reader holds that they conflict, the reading of this volume does not depend on resolving the conflict, since the further coming does not rest on either sentence alone."),
("ctbl", "The objections and the replies in one table.", [
    ("Objection", "Held by", "Reply in one line"),
    ("Malachi speaks of an office, and John filled it", "Stewart (2004); the Tabletalk devotional", "Granted for Luke 1:17; the future tense of Matthew 17:11 is spoken after John’s death"),
    ("“Elias is come already” settles it", "A common reading; none named", "It is the second of a pair that begins “shall first come”"),
    ("A further coming adds what Jesus did not say", "none named", "The Lord said “shall first come”; the volume adds no date, place or name"),
    ("“Restore all things” is John’s preaching", "none named", "The Gospels do not report all things restored; Acts 3:21 speaks of restitution as ahead"),
    ("John 1:21 and Matthew 11:14 contradict", "none named", "Each is read in its own setting; the reading does not rest on resolving them"),
    ("The promise is a further coming, as the volume holds", "Ice (2018); Kaiser (1982); Henebury (2021), in part", "Agreed in shape; the volume differs on the period, the person and any series"),
  ], [0.3, 0.3, 0.4]),
("sub", "12.7 What the volume takes from the replies"),
("bul", [
    "Ice, Kaiser and Henebury hold that a work of Elijah remains; the volume agrees in shape and differs on the period, the person and any series of forerunners.",
    "Stewart and the Tabletalk devotional are right that Luke 1:17 speaks of an office and a spirit, and right that John was not the returned Tishbite in the people’s sense.",
    "The office reading does not account for the future tense of Matthew 17:11 spoken after John’s death.",
    "“Elias is come already” is the second of a pair; the first sentence is “Elias truly shall first come, and restore all things.”",
    "John 1:21 and Matthew 11:14 are each read in their own setting, and the position does not rest on resolving them.",
  ]),
]
